# THE ERASURE INVARIANT (#86), machine-readable. `physical(s) = Erase(declaredKotlinType(s))` erases `Nullable(Tv)`
# to `object` at EVERY position, so a `Nullable(Tv)` node surviving anywhere in the emitted CIR IS the defect: a
# second physical representation of one Kotlin type, which at a value instantiation is a null pushed into a struct
# slot. The document drives every position at once — method param, method return, constructor param, field,
# property, body local, array element, nested generic argument, and a function type's return — so one negative
# assertion covers them all, and no positional carve-out can be reintroduced without reddening here.
-{"t":"nullable","of":{"t":"tv"
# A negative alone would also be satisfied by an empty CIR, or by an erasure to something other than `object`. These
# pin that each driven position actually became `object`: the method parameter, the constructor parameter, the field,
# the array element, the nested generic argument, and the function type's return.
+"name":"x","type":{"t":"fqn","name":"object"}
+"name":"slot","type":{"t":"fqn","name":"object"}
+"name":"cells","type":{"t":"array","elem":{"t":"fqn","name":"object"}}
+"name":"NullableTvProbeBox","args":[{"t":"fqn","name":"object"}]
+"ret":{"t":"fqn","name":"object"}
